; Record wire parsers — kernel/forms/helpers/record-parsers.form
;
; The application-level record parsers named in STUBS.md. Each reads
; the raw bytes of a sealed substance and validates them against the
; fixed-offset layout pinned in kernel/types/Records.md, producing the
; trusted typed record (or signalling malformation).
;
; This batch (A) encodes the two simplest, structurally-identical
; binary parsers — both TOTAL functions that return BOTTOM_HASH on
; malformed input:
;
;   S-06/parse_intent : (Bytes) → Intent or BOTTOM
;   S-08/parse_claim  : (Bytes) → Claim  or BOTTOM
;
; Their callers (S-06/match, S-08/check) JMPZ on `== BOTTOM_HASH` and
; map the failure into their own verdict (S-06's EILLFORMED trap;
; S-08's Reject result). Per kernel/types/Records.md § Parser totality
; contracts, NEITHER parser traps — getting this wrong would crash an
; act the design intends to reject gracefully.
;
; Wire model. Unlike S-07/parse_form (which parses canonical
; s-expression *text* into a ParsedForm), these records are sealed
; substances with the fixed binary layout of Records.md. "Parsing"
; here means: validate untrusted bytes against that layout, then
; extract each fixed-offset field and re-seal the trusted record. A
; deviation in length or type-tag prefix yields BOTTOM_HASH; a
; well-formed prefix guarantees every subsequent offset read is
; in-bounds, so the field reads never trap.
;
; Synthesis chain:
;   provocation:  STUBS.md catalogues S-06/parse_intent and
;                 S-08/parse_claim as pending. S-06/match and
;                 S-08/check READSLOT + CALL them on the bytes
;                 returned by READ of the intent / claim substance.
;   grounding:    A1.2 (type is intrinsic — the parser trusts and
;                 verifies the type tag), A6.1 (a unit of code is a
;                 substance), A0.7 (totality — the parser returns a
;                 defined result on every input, never diverges),
;                 A0.6 (code is thought, content-addressed).
;   approach:     fixed-offset binary validation. A shared header
;                 check (Parser/check_record_header) gates the field
;                 reads; the field reads (Parser/read_nat{4,8}_at,
;                 Parser/read_hash_at) are constant-offset slices.
;   composes_with:
;                 - S-03/ROOT_RO for the caller's READ (the parser
;                   itself takes already-read bytes; it holds
;                   S-03/ROOT_RO only to match the helper family's
;                   declared cap, and performs no READ of its own).
;                 - the four binary-field leaves, catalogued pending
;                   in STUBS.md (same layered convention as
;                   parser.form's Parser/* leaves).
;
; Totality note (load-bearing, mirrors Treap.md's contract note):
; check_record_header returning false is the ONLY path to BOTTOM, and
; the field-read leaves are total over an in-bounds (offset, len)
; — guaranteed in-bounds by a true header check. Therefore parse_intent
; and parse_claim are total and trap-free, exactly as their call sites
; require.

; ============================================================
; S-06/parse_intent — (Bytes) → Intent or BOTTOM
;
; Layout (Records.md § Intent, "Intent/v1", 177 bytes):
;   budget          Nat(8) @9
;   acceptance_form Hash   @17
;   goal            Hash   @49
;   parent          Hash   @81
;   inputs          Hash   @113   (sealed Vec{Hash})
;   constraints     Hash   @145   (sealed Vec{Hash})
; ============================================================

(form
  :type-tag      "Form/v1"
  :name          "parse_intent"
  :declared-caps (S-03/ROOT_RO)
  :declared-traps ()
  :arity         1
  :locals-n      7
  :code (
    STORE   0                       ; locals[0] := input bytes

    ;; Validate length (177) and type-tag prefix ("Intent/v1").
    LOAD    0
    PUSH    "Intent/v1"
    PUSH    177
    PUSH    "Parser/check_record_header"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=3                ; → Bool (well-formed prefix + length)
    JMPZ    +bad                    ; malformed → BOTTOM_HASH

    ;; Extract each field at its fixed offset.
    LOAD    0
    PUSH    9
    PUSH    "Parser/read_nat8_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → budget

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    17
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → acceptance_form

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    49
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → goal

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    81
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → parent

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    113
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → inputs

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    145
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → constraints

    MAKEVEC 6                       ; [budget, acceptance_form, goal, parent, inputs, constraints]
    SEAL    Intent
    RET

    :bad
    PUSH    BOTTOM_HASH
    RET
  )
  :rationale-hash $$BLAKE3$$/record-parsers-rationale
)

; ============================================================
; S-08/parse_claim — (Bytes) → Claim or BOTTOM
;
; Layout (Records.md § Claim, "Claim/v1", 108 bytes):
;   invariant_id     Nat(4) @8
;   form_hash_before Hash   @12
;   form_hash_after  Hash   @44
;   env              Hash   @76
; ============================================================

(form
  :type-tag      "Form/v1"
  :name          "parse_claim"
  :declared-caps (S-03/ROOT_RO)
  :declared-traps ()
  :arity         1
  :locals-n      5
  :code (
    STORE   0                       ; locals[0] := input bytes

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    "Claim/v1"
    PUSH    108
    PUSH    "Parser/check_record_header"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=3
    JMPZ    +bad

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    8
    PUSH    "Parser/read_nat4_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → invariant_id

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    12
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → form_hash_before

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    44
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → form_hash_after

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    76
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → env

    MAKEVEC 4                       ; [invariant_id, form_hash_before, form_hash_after, env]
    SEAL    Claim
    RET

    :bad
    PUSH    BOTTOM_HASH
    RET
  )
  :rationale-hash $$BLAKE3$$/record-parsers-rationale
)

; ============================================================
; S-08/parse_proof — (Bytes) → Proof or BOTTOM
;
; Layout (Records.md § Proof, "Proof/v1", 72 bytes):
;   claim_hash Hash @8
;   rule_tree  Hash @40   (root ProofNode hash)
;
; TOTAL: S-08/check is declared-traps () — a parse failure becomes a
; Reject result, so this parser returns BOTTOM_HASH on malformed input
; and never traps. (Records.md § Parser totality contracts.)
; ============================================================

(form
  :type-tag      "Form/v1"
  :name          "parse_proof"
  :declared-caps (S-03/ROOT_RO)
  :declared-traps ()
  :arity         1
  :locals-n      3
  :code (
    STORE   0                       ; locals[0] := input bytes

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    "Proof/v1"
    PUSH    72
    PUSH    "Parser/check_record_header"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=3
    JMPZ    +bad

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    8
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → claim_hash

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    40
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → rule_tree

    MAKEVEC 2                       ; [claim_hash, rule_tree]
    SEAL    Proof
    RET

    :bad
    PUSH    BOTTOM_HASH
    RET
  )
  :rationale-hash $$BLAKE3$$/record-parsers-rationale
)

; ============================================================
; S-07/parse_exec_state — (Bytes) → ExecState  (TRAPS ETYPE)
;
; Layout (Records.md § ExecState, "ExecState/v1", 240 bytes); field
; order fixed by S-07-form-runtime.form's MAKEVEC 8:
;   form_hash    Hash   @12
;   pc           Nat(4) @44
;   locals       Hash   @48    (sealed Vec{Hash})
;   stack        Hash   @80    (sealed Vec{Hash})
;   cap_view     Hash   @112   (sealed Vec{CapId})
;   weave_prev   Hash   @144
;   inputs_hash  Hash   @176
;   attention_id Hash   @208
;
; UNLIKE the other binary parsers, this one TRAPS ETYPE on malformed
; input rather than returning BOTTOM. S-07/resume consumes the result
; directly (no BOTTOM check) — it restores a continuation it sealed
; itself, so a malformed continuation is a genuine integrity fault,
; not a graceful-reject case. (Records.md § Parser totality contracts.)
; ============================================================

(form
  :type-tag      "Form/v1"
  :name          "parse_exec_state"
  :declared-caps (S-03/ROOT_RO)
  :declared-traps (ETYPE)
  :arity         1
  :locals-n      9
  :code (
    STORE   0                       ; locals[0] := input bytes

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    "ExecState/v1"
    PUSH    240
    PUSH    "Parser/check_record_header"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=3
    JMPZ    +trap_bad               ; malformed → TRAP ETYPE

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    12
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → form_hash

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    44
    PUSH    "Parser/read_nat4_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → pc

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    48
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → locals

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    80
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → stack

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    112
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → cap_view

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    144
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → weave_prev

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    176
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → inputs_hash

    LOAD    0
    PUSH    208
    PUSH    "Parser/read_hash_at"
    SEAL    Hash
    READSLOT
    CALL    h=$ /n=2                ; → attention_id

    MAKEVEC 8                       ; [form_hash, pc, locals, stack, cap_view, weave_prev, inputs_hash, attention_id]
    SEAL    ExecState
    RET

    :trap_bad
    TRAP    ETYPE
  )
  :rationale-hash $$BLAKE3$$/record-parsers-rationale
)

(exports
  (parse_intent     (Bytes) → Hash)    ; → Intent hash,    or BOTTOM_HASH on malformed
  (parse_claim      (Bytes) → Hash)    ; → Claim hash,     or BOTTOM_HASH on malformed
  (parse_proof      (Bytes) → Hash)    ; → Proof hash,     or BOTTOM_HASH on malformed
  (parse_exec_state (Bytes) → Hash))   ; → ExecState hash, or TRAP ETYPE on malformed

; ============================================================
; Pending sub-helpers (binary-field leaves)
;
; Shared by every fixed-offset binary record parser — parse_intent,
; parse_claim, parse_proof, parse_exec_state (this file), and the
; parse_provocation batch to follow. Each is small and total over its
; inputs; catalogued in STUBS.md as pending and encoded in a sibling
; batch, the same layered convention parser.form uses for its Parser/*
; leaves.
;
;   - Parser/check_record_header (Bytes, Bytes tag, Nat len) → Bool
;       true iff len(bytes) == len AND bytes start with the tag literal.
;       The single gate to BOTTOM; a true result guarantees every
;       field offset below is in-bounds.
;   - Parser/read_hash_at  (Bytes, Nat offset) → Hash
;       slice the 32 bytes at offset and SEAL Hash.
;   - Parser/read_nat4_at  (Bytes, Nat offset) → Nat
;       decode the 4 big-endian bytes at offset.
;   - Parser/read_nat8_at  (Bytes, Nat offset) → Nat
;       decode the 8 big-endian bytes at offset.
;
; These three readers are total over an in-bounds (bytes, offset)
; — and in-boundedness is exactly what a true check_record_header
; guarantees — so the three total parsers never trap, and
; parse_exec_state's only trap is the explicit TRAP ETYPE on a false
; header check (its declared trap, matching its call site's trust model).
;
; Proof obligations. The parser bodies range from ~25 (parse_proof) to
; ~85 (parse_exec_state, 8 fields) IL instructions — over the
; ~30-instruction smallness threshold of synthesis/PROTOCOL.md § Helper
; exemption, so this file is NOT exempt and its .proof artifact
; (kernel/forms/helpers/record-parsers.proof)
; is a tracked follow-up, the same status as parser.form / trie.form /
; forest.form / treap.form (obligations listed inline now, artifact
; deferred; tracked in STUBS.md § Non-exempt helpers requiring proofs).
; The obligations:
;   1. Total: every parser terminates with a defined result — a sealed
;      record hash, BOTTOM_HASH (parse_intent/claim/proof), or the single
;      TRAP ETYPE (parse_exec_state); never diverges. (Structural: the
;      only non-linear control is the single JMPZ to a PUSH BOTTOM_HASH;
;      RET, or — for parse_exec_state — to TRAP ETYPE.)
;   2. Layout-faithful: the offsets read match Records.md row-for-row
;      (177-byte Intent / 108-byte Claim / 72-byte Proof /
;      240-byte ExecState).
;   3. Reject-on-deviation: any input whose length or type-tag prefix
;      differs from the layout yields BOTTOM_HASH (the three total
;      parsers) or TRAP ETYPE (parse_exec_state), via check_record_header.
; ============================================================
